# Response headers for the static site (Cloudflare Workers static assets: https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/).
# Astro copies this file to dist/_headers. /api/* never reaches these rules: the Worker answers it and sets its own
# headers (worker/src/http.ts: CSP default-src 'none', nosniff, no-store, ...).
#
# Content-Security-Policy is split in two, and browsers enforce BOTH:
#   1. Every page carries a <meta http-equiv="content-security-policy"> (astro.config.mjs security.csp, completed by
#      integrations/csp-hashes.mjs) with default-src 'self', the third-party hosts below, and a script-src/style-src
#      that lists the SHA-256 of exactly the inline scripts and styles of that page, with no 'unsafe-inline'.
#   2. This header adds what a <meta> policy cannot express (frame-ancestors) plus the structural directives again.
#      It deliberately has no default-src/script-src/style-src: a header can't list per-page hashes, so any fetch
#      directive here would have to be looser ('unsafe-inline') or would block the pages' own hashed inline code.
#      Leaving them out means the stricter per-page <meta> alone governs scripts and styles.
# Third-party origins the pages may use (allowed in the <meta>), and nothing else:
#   challenges.cloudflare.com      Turnstile (script + iframe), contact form and chat
#   static.cloudflareinsights.com  Web Analytics beacon script; cloudflareinsights.com receives its reports
# If a Cloudflare feature that injects inline scripts is switched on (Rocket Loader, Email Obfuscation, Zaraz, Bot
# Fight Mode JS detections), the <meta> policy refuses its snippet: check the live console before enabling one.
# Rules are additive on Cloudflare (a header set by two matching rules is joined with a comma), so no rule below
# repeats a header from "/*" except Content-Security-Policy on *.svg, where two policies are intended (both apply).
# HSTS without `preload` for now: add it (and submit to hstspreload.org) once every subdomain is known to be HTTPS-only.
# Violations of the header policy are reported to the Worker (POST /api/csp-report, worker/src/csp-report.ts: an
# anonymous counter, nothing about the visitor): report-to + Reporting-Endpoints (Chrome 96+, Safari 16.4+, Firefox
# 149+; the path must start with "/"), and report-uri for older browsers (ignored wherever report-to works). A <meta>
# policy can't report, so the build appends one rule per page (scripts/postbuild/early-hints.mjs) with a
# Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only copy of that page's <meta> policy (its hashes included, minus the directives this
# header already reports): the browser then reports whatever the <meta> blocks, from the first byte of the page and
# without any script. That rule also carries the page's Early Hints Link header.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; report-uri /api/csp-report; report-to csp
  Reporting-Endpoints: csp="/api/csp-report"
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), browsing-topics=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# Fingerprinted build output (file names change with their content): cache for a year, never revalidate. Only this
# site's pages use the scripts, styles, fonts and images, so other sites can't embed them (CORP). Sharing images,
# icons, the photo and the CV PDFs stay embeddable: social platforms and other sites load those.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin

# SVG files opened directly can't run script or load anything (they are only used as images and CSS masks).
/*.svg
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; sandbox

# Sharing images: re-rendered on deploy when their text changes; social platforms cache them on their side anyway.
/og/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

# The generated CV PDFs change with every content update: short cache, revalidate.
/cv/*.pdf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# Text files for crawlers and AI assistants: Workers static assets send "text/plain" without a charset, and these
# contain non-ASCII characters ("–", "·"). One rule per file sets the full value (it replaces the default; never set
# Content-Type in a second matching rule, or the values are joined).
/robots.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
/llms.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
/llms-full.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
# RFC 9116 requires text/plain with charset=utf-8 (/security.txt redirects here: postbuild/redirects.mjs).
/.well-known/security.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# "Add to contacts" (src/pages/mohamed-hatem.vcf.ts, generated from content/profile.ts). The type is set here rather than
# left to the extension (.vcf maps to text/x-vcard in some MIME tables): iOS Safari opens text/vcard as a contact card,
# Android hands it to Contacts. One rule, so the Content-Type replaces the default.
/mohamed-hatem.vcf
  Content-Type: text/vcard; charset=utf-8
  Content-Disposition: inline; filename="Mohamed-Hatem.vcf"
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# Icons, the logo masks and the profile photo used in structured data: stable, unversioned file names.
/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/apple-touch-icon.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/icon-*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/site.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/ar.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/logos/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
/mohamed-hatem.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
